{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And all the people saw the sounds, and they said to Moses</b> The opinion of the commentators is that this was after the giving of the Torah, as it says (Deuteronomy 5:20–22), \"And you came near to me, all the heads of your tribes and your elders, and you said, 'Behold, HaShem our Elohim has shown us… If we continue to hear the voice of HaShem any more, we shall die'\" etc. But this is not my opinion, because here it says, \"Let not Elohim speak with us,\" and it does not say \"anymore\"; and Moses said here, \"Do not fear,\" whereas there it is stated (Deuteronomy 5:25), \"They have done well in all that they have spoken.\" Furthermore, here it does not recount that they were frightened except by the sounds and the torches and by the mountain, which was smoking, whereas there they were frightened by the speech of the Shekhinah itself, as they said, \"For who is there of all flesh, who has heard the voice of the living Elohim speaking out of the midst of the fire, as we have, and lived?\" (Deuteronomy 5:23). Moreover, here (Exodus 20:21) it says, \"And Moses drew near to the thick darkness,\" and it does not say that he entered into it.\n\nNow what seems correct to me regarding the section and the arrangement of the matter is that <b>\"and all the people saw… and said to Moses\"</b> occurred before the giving of the Torah. At the outset, Scripture mentioned in order all the words of Elohim — what He commanded Moses concerning the bounding of the mountain and the warning to the people — and the Ten Commandments. Now it goes back and mentions the words of the people to Moses, and says that from the time they saw the sounds and the torches, they recoiled backward and stood at a distance, beyond the boundary of the mountain that Moses had bounded.\n\nAnd the sequence of events is as follows: In the morning there were sounds and lightning and a loud shofar blast (Exodus 19:16), and the Shekhinah had not yet descended — as in the verse, \"And a great and strong wind rending the mountains and breaking in pieces the rocks before HaShwH — but HaShem was not in the wind\" (1 Kings 19:11) — and the people trembled in the camp at their stations (Exodus 19:16). Moses strengthened their hearts and brought them out to meet Elohim, and they stationed themselves at the foot of the mountain (Exodus 19:17). While they were there at the foot of the mountain, watching and taking their stand, HaShem descended upon the mountain in fire (Exodus 19:18), and its smoke ascended to the heart of the heavens — darkness, cloud, and thick darkness (Deuteronomy 4:11) — and the mountain itself trembled and shook (Exodus 19:18), as things move in the quake called \"zalzalah\" or even more than that. Thus it is written (Psalms 114:4), \"The mountains danced like rams, the hills like lambs,\" and this is not a metaphor, just as it is no metaphor that \"The sea saw and fled, the Jordan turned backward\" (Psalms 114:3). And the sound of the shofar grew exceedingly strong (Exodus 19:19). Then the people saw and recoiled backward and stood at a distance (here), beyond the boundary, and then they all said to him that Elohim should not speak with them at all, lest they die — for at the vision their pains turned upon them and they retained no strength (Daniel 10:16), and if they were to hear the speech they would die. But Moses strengthened them and said to them, \"Do not fear\" (Exodus 20:20), and they listened to him, and the people stood at a distance in their station (Exodus 20:21), for they refused in any way to come near the boundary. And Moses drew near to the thick darkness (ibid.) — he did not enter into it. And then Elohim spoke the Ten Commandments.\n\nAfter the Ten Commandments, Scripture did not mention here what the elders said to Moses, because it wanted to explain the commandments and the statutes in order. But in Deuteronomy (Deuteronomy 5:20), Moses mentioned that after the Commandments, all the heads of their tribes and their elders came near to him and said, \"If we continue to hear the voice of HaShem our Elohim any more, we shall die\" — for they reckoned in their souls that they would no longer be able to bear the weight of the word of HaShem our Elohim, for they thought that Elohim wished to speak all the commandments directly to them. Therefore they said, \"You draw near and hear all that HaShem our Elohim will say, and you shall speak to us, and we will hear and do\" (Deuteronomy 5:24). And the Holy One, blessed be He, acknowledged their words and said, \"They have done well in all that they have spoken,\" for such was the desire before Him — that He not make them hear more than the Ten Commandments — and the fear was upright before Him." "<b>And they recoiled</b> According to the opinion of our Sages (Mekhilta here), נֹעַ here means nothing but trembling, as it says (Isaiah 24:20), \"The earth shall stagger to and fro like a drunkard.\" If so, the verse means that they shook, and out of their terror they drew further back and stood at a distance. According to the opinion of the literal exegetes, <b>וַיָּנֻעוּ</b> means they moved from their place backward and stood at a distance, from the language of \"נָע וָנָד תִּהְיֶה בָאָרֶץ\" — \"a fugitive and a wanderer you shall be in the land\" (Genesis 4:12), and likewise \"וַיְנִעֵם בַּמִּדְבָּר\" — \"and He made them wander in the wilderness\" (Numbers 32:13)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Deuteronomy 5:22 with the phrase וָמָתְנוּ ('and we shall die') which actually appears in 5:21 in standard verse numbering; the citation 5:20-22 covers the full speech. The citation of Genesis 4:2 for 'נע ונד' is in fact Genesis 4:14 in all standard editions; the verse reference is preserved as Ramban gives it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:17:45.029893833Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}